<section class="sub" id="flagship">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">02 · MOST-CITED NATIONALLY</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Flagship reforms</h2>
<p class="section-note">Large cities whose reforms set the national template — useful for the scale of ambition and the data they've generated, less useful as direct physical/demographic comparisons for Santa Fe.</p>
</div>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="compare cardtable">
<thead>
<tr><th>City</th><th>What they did</th><th>Sources</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr id="pcs-minneapolis">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/minneapolis/">Minneapolis<span class="place">Minnesota</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Adopted 2018 · effective 2020</span></td>
<td>
<p>First major U.S. city to end single-family-only zoning citywide. Paired duplex/triplex legalization with phased parking-minimum elimination (2009–2021) and transit-corridor upzoning (3–6 stories on corridors, 10–30 near light rail).</p>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>~21,000</b> units permitted, 2017–22</span>
<span class="stat"><b>+12%</b> housing stock vs. 4% statewide</span>
<span class="stat"><b>1% rent growth</b> vs. 14% statewide</span>
</div>
<p class="muted">Caveat: 87% of new units were in 20+ unit buildings — the duplex/triplex legalization alone drove only ~1% of permits. The parking and corridor moves did most of the production work.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2024/01/04/minneapolis-land-use-reforms-offer-a-blueprint-for-housing-affordability">Pew Charitable Trusts ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.minneapolisfed.org/article/2025/unpacking-supply-and-demand-in-rent-trends-since-the-minneapolis-2040-plan">Minneapolis Fed ↗</a>
<a href="https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/comprehensive-zoning-reform-in-minneapolis-mn/">Bipartisan Policy Center ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-portland">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/portland/">Portland<span class="place">Oregon</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">RIP 2021 · RIP2 2022</span></td>
<td>
<p>Residential Infill Project legalized duplex through fourplex (six units if income-restricted), ADUs, and cottage clusters across zones covering 75% of the city's residential land. RIP2 added rowhouses and larger-lot flexibility.</p>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>1,400+</b> units permitted, Aug 2021–Jun 2024</span>
<span class="stat"><b>43%</b> of citywide permits from single-dwelling zones (up from 10–20%)</span>
<span class="stat"><b>$200–300k</b> cheaper than new detached homes</span>
</div>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.portland.gov/bps/planning/rip2/news/2025/2/4/portland-sees-significant-production-middle-housing-resulting">Portland.gov results ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.portland.gov/bps/planning/rip2/about">Portland.gov RIP2 overview ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-sacramento">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/sacramento/">Sacramento<span class="place">California</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">2040 General Plan · adopted 2024</span></td>
<td>
<p>Missing Middle Housing ordinance paired with the 2040 General Plan — called by Opticos Design "one of the most progressive missing middle strategies in the U.S." As of August 2026, local reporting finds construction still lagging well behind legalization.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://opticosdesign.com/blog/sacramento-adopts-one-of-the-most-progressive-missing-middle-strategies-in-the-u-s/">Opticos Design ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.capradio.org/articles/2026/08/07/missing-middle-housing-still-mostly-missing-in-sacramento/">CapRadio, Aug 2026 ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-charlotte">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/charlotte/">Charlotte<span class="place">North Carolina</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">UDO adopted June 2023</span></td>
<td>
<p>Replaced case-by-case rezoning petitions with by-right duplex/triplex/quadplex approval, and held firm through a 2024 council review of the triplex provisions prompted by neighborhood traffic and school-crowding concerns.</p>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>8,537</b> duplex/triplex/quad units submitted or approved in 3 years</span>
<span class="stat">vs. "hundreds" pre-UDO</span>
</div>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.wfae.org/business/2026-08-13/udo-three-years-later-how-many-duplexes-triplexes-and-quads-have-been-built">WFAE, three years later ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.wfae.org/politics/2024-04-16/going-backwards-charlotte-prepares-to-rescind-controversial-triplex-rules">WFAE, 2024 council review ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-austin">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/austin/">Austin<span class="place">Texas</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">HOME Phase 1 (Dec 2023) · Phase 2 (May 2024)</span></td>
<td>
<p>Allows up to three units on most single-family lots; Phase 2 cut minimum lot sizes (to ~1,800 sq ft in many areas) so small-lot infill actually pencils out financially, rather than only rezoning to multifamily.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.statesman.com/story/news/local/2024/05/17/austin-city-council-home-phase-2-land-development-code-reducing-minimum-lot-size/73677616007/">Austin American-Statesman ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.austintexas.gov/development-services/home-amendments">City of Austin ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-berkeley">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/berkeley/">Berkeley<span class="place">California</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Committed 2021–22 · follow-on package 2024–</span></td>
<td>
<p>Symbolically significant: the city that invented single-family zoning in 1916 voted to end it. A follow-on package moving through 2024–25 would allow taller, denser housing citywide beyond the initial repeal.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2021/02/112396-history-unmade-berkeley-city-council-votes-eliminate-single-family-zoning">Planetizen, 2021 vote ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2024/08/131321-birthplace-single-family-zoning-poised-pass-upzoning-plan">Planetizen, 2024 follow-on ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-cincinnati">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/cincinnati/">Cincinnati<span class="place">Ohio</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Connected Communities · adopted 2024</span></td>
<td>
<p>Ties zoning reform directly to transit: reduced parking minimums and increased density along transit lines and commercial corridors. The multi-hearing rollout offers real, instructive lessons for engagement design.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.wvxu.org/politics/2024-01-29/aftab-pureval-zoning-reform-housing-density-connected-communities">WVXU overview ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2024/05/129126-cincinnatis-controversial-zoning-plan-clears-first-hurdle">Planetizen ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-columbus">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/columbus/">Columbus<span class="place">Ohio</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">"Zone In" · first phase passed 2024</span></td>
<td>
<p>First rewrite of Columbus's zoning code in decades, explicitly built around missing-middle typologies that the old code made nearly impossible to build by right.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.nbc4i.com/news/local-news/columbus/why-columbus-city-officials-are-looking-to-overhaul-zoning-code/">NBC4 ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2023/04/122711-columbus-rewriting-its-zoning-code">Planetizen ↗</a></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="cautionary">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">03 · REFORM DURABILITY</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Reform durability — what makes a reform last</h2>
<p class="section-note">Several of the highest-profile reforms nationally have faced reversal or litigation — valuable evidence for building a Santa Fe approach that holds up.</p>
</div>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="compare cardtable">
<thead>
<tr><th>City</th><th>What they did</th><th>Sources</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="clay" id="pcs-gainesville">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/gainesville/">Gainesville<span class="place">Florida</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Repealed Oct 2022 · reversed Apr 2023</span></td>
<td>
<p>Eliminated single-family zoning citywide in 2022; a change in council composition brought most of it back in 2023 — a clear illustration of why anchoring a reform in the comprehensive plan, with durable public buy-in, helps it last.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2022/10/119264-gainesville-eliminates-single-family-zoning">Planetizen, 2022 repeal ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.planetizen.com/news/2023/04/122742-gainesville-reverses-zoning-reforms-reinstates-single-family-zoning">Planetizen, 2023 reversal ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="clay" id="pcs-arlington">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/arlington/">Arlington County<span class="place">Virginia</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Adopted 2023 · reinstated on appeal Jun 2025</span></td>
<td>
<p>"Expanded Housing Options" (up to 6 units/lot) faced a legal challenge that paused missing-middle permits for roughly a year; a Court of Appeals decision reinstated the policy in June 2025, with the case potentially still headed to the state Supreme Court.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.arlnow.com/2025/06/25/breaking-appeals-court-puts-missing-middle-back-on-the-books-in-arlington/">ARLnow ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.yimbylaw.org/law-journal/arlington-missing-middle-case-to-be-reviewed-by-the-va-supreme-court-sort-of">YIMBY Law ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr class="clay" id="pcs-alexandria">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/alexandria/">Alexandria<span class="place">Virginia</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Adopted 2023–24 · upheld Nov 2025</span></td>
<td>
<p>"Zoning for Housing" ended single-family-only zoning in 2023–24; a legal challenge filed in January 2024 was dismissed in November 2025, upholding the reform.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.washingtonpost.com/dc-md-va/2024/08/26/alexandria-zoning-housing-lawsuit-trial/">Washington Post ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.alxnow.com/2025/11/12/just-in-judge-dismisses-case-against-alexandrias-zoning-for-housing-overhaul/">ALXnow, dismissal ↗</a></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p class="section-note" style="margin-top:18px;"><strong style="color:var(--ink);">Takeaway:</strong> the most durable reforms pair the zoning change with a defensible comprehensive-plan basis, sustained engagement <em>before</em> adoption, and simple, objective standards — a combination well within reach for Santa Fe's update.</p>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="peer">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">04 · CLOSER TO SANTA FE'S SCALE</div>
<h2 class="section-title">Peer-scale cities</h2>
<p class="section-note">Smaller populations, historic districts, tourism or university economies, mountain / high-desert geography, and similar "neighborhood character" politics — more useful as direct comparables than the flagships above.</p>
</div>
<div class="table-scroll">
<table class="compare cardtable">
<thead>
<tr><th>City</th><th>What they did</th><th>Sources</th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr id="pcs-boulder">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/boulder/">Boulder<span class="place">Colorado</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">In progress, 2024–</span></td>
<td>
<p>Home-rule college town with an entrenched growth-boundary and height-limit tradition much like Santa Fe's. "Zoning for Affordable Housing" and expanding ADU rules now layer on top of Colorado's 2024 statewide land-use package, which preempted some local restrictions — a model for how state pressure can do political work local council would otherwise absorb alone.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://bouldercolorado.gov/projects/zoning-affordable-housing">City of Boulder ↗</a>
<a href="https://boulderreportinglab.org/2024/05/09/how-5-new-colorado-land-use-measures-will-impact-the-city-of-boulder/">Boulder Reporting Lab ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-missoula">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/missoula/">Missoula<span class="place">Montana</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Growth policy adopted Dec 2024</span></td>
<td>
<p>Directly comparable: university town, mountain-valley setting, tourism economy, acute housing-cost crisis. "Our Missoula" is now driving a full zoning-code rewrite; an October 2025 draft would allow apartments in most of the city. The process has deliberately emphasized resident involvement — worth studying the engagement model specifically.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://montanafreepress.org/2025/10/10/missoulas-draft-zoning-code-allows-apartments-in-most-of-the-city/">Montana Free Press, draft code ↗</a>
<a href="https://montanafreepress.org/2024/08/02/getting-residents-involved-in-zoning-reform-in-missoula-montana/">Montana Free Press, engagement ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-bend">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/bend/">Bend<span class="place">Oregon</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Climate Friendly Areas, 2023–</span></td>
<td>
<p>Fast-growing high-desert resort city implementing Oregon's statewide Climate-Friendly and Equitable Communities rules — designated walkable, mixed-use "Climate Friendly Areas" with reduced parking mandates, on top of state-required middle-housing code. A model for concentrating walkable-center intensity rather than upzoning uniformly.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.bendoregon.gov/government/departments/growth-management/climate-friendly-and-equitable-communities">City of Bend ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.centraloregonlandwatch.org/update/2025/11/19/big-moves-on-housing-and-smart-urbanization">Central Oregon LandWatch ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-flagstaff">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/flagstaff/">Flagstaff<span class="place">Arizona</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Middle-housing code, 2024–25</span></td>
<td>
<p><strong>Likely the closest national peer to Santa Fe</strong> — similar population, historic downtown, university presence, high-desert setting, severe housing-cost burden, heavy tourism. Arizona's HB 2721 forced Flagstaff to legalize duplex–fourplex housing near its core; the resulting citywide code drew broad public support at hearings, unlike comparable proposals elsewhere. Flagstaff's code was already form-based, a useful precedent for historic-character concerns.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://citizenportal.ai/articles/7006447/Flagstaff-City/Coconino-County/Arizona/Flagstaff-council-moves-forward-with-citywide-middle-housing-code-public-hearing-draws-broad-support">CitizenPortal ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.azleague.org/DocumentCenter/View/28045/HB2721-middle-housing">AZ League model ordinance ↗</a>
<a href="https://formbasedcodes.org/codes/flagstaff-zoning-code/">Form-Based Codes Institute ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-spokane">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/spokane/">Spokane<span class="place">Washington</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Permanent code adopted Nov 2023</span></td>
<td>
<p>The best-documented model for middle-housing dimensional standards: no max density under 2 acres, 1,200 sq ft minimum lot, 65–80% lot coverage, 40-ft height limit, generous alley ADUs, parking waived within a half-mile of transit. CNU and Strong Towns hold it up as the national standard for simple, objective code language over discretionary review.</p>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>455+</b> units permitted under interim rules alone</span>
</div>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.theurbanist.org/2023/11/30/spokanes-permanent-middle-housing-rules-should-set-a-statewide-standard/">The Urbanist ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.cnu.org/publicsquare/2023/02/15/spokane-streamlines-missing-middle-housing">CNU ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-tacoma">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/tacoma/">Tacoma<span class="place">Washington</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Home in Tacoma · 2021–24</span></td>
<td>
<p>Allows duplex through sixplex citywide, scaled to proximity to transit and centers, alongside a comprehensive-plan target of 325,000 residents by 2040.</p>
<div class="stat-row">
<span class="stat"><b>+62%</b> proposed housing units in the first year</span>
</div>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://tacoma.gov/news/home-in-tacoma-sparks-62-increase-in-number-of-proposed-housing-units-in-first-year/">City of Tacoma ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.theurbanist.org/2025/02/19/tacoma-aims-for-325000-residents-by-2040/">The Urbanist ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-southbend">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/southbend/">South Bend<span class="place">Indiana</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Infill design catalog, 2022</span></td>
<td>
<p>A smaller, lower-cost-of-living city that took a different tack: instead of only rewriting zoning code, it pre-approved a catalog of missing-middle and small-multifamily building designs, so builders skip a chunk of design and permitting friction. Indianapolis has since copied the approach.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.strongtowns.org/journal/2022-10-6-pre-approved-house-designs-jump-start-infill-development-in-south-bend">Strong Towns ↗</a>
<a href="https://southbendin.gov/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/SBBT_Catalog_23-0506-lowres.pdf">South Bend infill catalog (PDF) ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-santabarbara">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/santabarbara/">Santa Barbara<span class="place">California</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">Housing Element, 2023–2031</span></td>
<td>
<p>The closest comparable among historic, tourism-economy coastal cities with strict design-review traditions. Uses a "suitable sites inventory" and unit-lot subdivision tools to fit multiple smaller units on sites that still read as one historic-scaled building — directly relevant to Santa Fe's historic-district overlay concerns.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://santabarbaraca.gov/HousingElement">City of Santa Barbara ↗</a>
<a href="https://santabarbaraca.gov/government/priorities-policies/housing-initiatives/housing-element-update/suitable-sites-inventory">Suitable Sites Inventory ↗</a></td>
</tr>
<tr id="pcs-somerville">
<td class="city"><a href="/cities/somerville/">Somerville<span class="place">Massachusetts</span></a><span class="tagline-cell">SomerVision 2040 rewrite, 2019</span></td>
<td>
<p>Not a physical or economic peer, but the most-cited "state of the art code for a walkable city" (CNU's phrase). Ties allowed density directly to walkability and transit-access metrics rather than blanket citywide rules — a useful structural model if Santa Fe wants to concentrate intensity around specific corridors and nodes.</p>
</td>
<td class="src-cell"><a href="https://www.cnu.org/publicsquare/2020/06/04/state-art-code-walkable-city">CNU ↗</a>
<a href="https://www.somervillema.gov/news/somerville-city-council-administration-pass-citys-first-zoning-overhaul-30-years">City of Somerville ↗</a></td>
</tr>
